<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benjamin Disraeli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–<a href="/years/1881">1881</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Earl of Beaconsfield</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Benjamin Disraeli was a British Conservative statesman and novelist who served as prime minister in 1868 and from 1874 to 1880. He carried the Second Reform Act of 1867, bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> a large stake in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> Company, had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> proclaimed Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and represented Britain at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> in 1878; he was created Earl of Beaconsfield in 1876.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benjamin-disraeli.jpg" alt="Benjamin Disraeli" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cornelius Jabez Hughes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benjamin_Disraeli,_Earl_of_Beaconsfield_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
